        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Values to remember"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A few angles appear again and again: 0, 30, 45, 60 and 90 degrees. Learn their values once and a whole chapter becomes easy."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "0 and 90",
          "30 degrees",
          "60 degrees",
          "sec^2 = 1 + tan^2",
          "sin 45 = cos 45 = 1/root 2",
          "sin(90 - A) = cos A",
          "sin^2 A + cos^2 A = 1",
          "sine list"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "For 45 degrees, sin = cos = 1 by root 2 and tan = 1.\nFor 30 degrees, sin = 1/2, cos = root 3 by 2 and tan = 1 by root 3.\nFor 60 degrees, sin = root 3 by 2, cos = 1/2 and tan = root 3.\nFor 0 degrees: sin = 0, cos = 1, tan = 0. For 90 degrees: sin = 1, cos = 0 and tan is not defined.\nA memory trick for sine: 0, 1/2, 1/root 2, root 3/2, 1 for the angles 0, 30, 45, 60, 90. Cosine is the same list reversed.\nIdentity one: sin squared A + cos squared A = 1, true for every angle A.\nIdentity two: sec squared A = 1 + tan squared A, and identity three: cosec squared A = 1 + cot squared A.\nComplementary angles: sin(90 - A) = cos A and cos(90 - A) = sin A, so sin 60 = cos 30."
        }
      },
